+# AHAS 1.0.4: stored sensitivity results remain visible
+
+This presentation release addresses AHAS-RW-001 from the independently reviewed 1.0.3 pilot. The accepted first pilot remains preliminary evidence. Its inputs, protocols, results, scores and receipts are historical and are not rewritten by this repair.
+
+## Milestone checklist
+
+- [x] Read the user's staged request and independent review; preserve the analytical baseline.
+- [x] Reproduce the missing public sensitivity overview before changing the renderer.
+- [x] Add stored per-setting/per-stream counts and inspectable candidate intervals.
+- [x] Pass synthetic and unchanged private regressions, safety, ordering and saved-JSON replay.
+- [x] Pass reference tests, original audit regressions and complete canonical recomputation.
+- [x] Generate separate corrected private presentations and a public synthetic report.
+- [x] Verify analytical preservation and installed production wheel.
+- [x] Verify final release archive.
+- [x] Close Stage A before freezing/scoring the next evaluation protocol.
+
+## Presentation contract
+
+The overview in `src/account_history_analyzer/reporting.py:render_markdown` traverses `style.streams` in exported list order, then `sensitivity.penalty_settings` in predefined list order. It joins stored change results and matching rows by stream/setting identifiers. Mapping insertion order has no meaning. The renderer does not call feature extraction, matching or optimization.
+
+Candidate count is the number of exported boundary objects. Primary-matched count is the number of stored one-to-one matching pairs. Unmatched-setting candidate count is the number of stored unmatched setting boundary indices. An executed `ok` or `no_measurable_variation` result can have zero candidates. A skipped/insufficient result shows unavailable counts; an executed setting with an unavailable primary shows not-comparable matching counts. Disabled alternative constructions retain their separate `not_run` status and `disabled_by_configuration` reason without inventing a new penalty-result enum.
+
+Each setting with candidates has a disclosure containing the stored original-record interval, source endpoint IDs, endpoint times and full adjacent window times. These intervals do not identify an exact event date, author change, significance or confidence. Existing primary matching details remain visible. Anchors combine the setting, stream and boundary IDs through a digest; source values use the existing inert text escaping. These details add no source prose excerpts. Both excerpt modes preserve the established n-gram disclosure rule and lossless label notation.
+
+RW-002 receives a small terminology clarification: the existing `unsafe_scheme` parser category includes unsupported/scheme-less destinations and does not establish maliciousness. RW-003 remains an explicit scope limit: body/title text does not include native post destination metadata outside the input contract. Neither the parser nor input model changes. RW-004 runtime/memory/output cost, stable-zero feature presentation and a shorter overview remain separate work.
+
+## Release identities and preservation
+
+Package/suite and report template versions become 1.0.4. The strict reuse-accounting release guard recognizes 1.0.4; legacy compatibility and all typed payload fields stay intact. The implementation fingerprint and report-template resource hash change transparently. Numerical source, default thresholds, method registry, feature definitions, evidence selection, pinned PR #383 optimizer and dependency/resource pins remain unchanged.
+
+Fresh analytical comparisons permit differences only at `analysis.suite_version`, `analysis.implementation_fingerprint` and `analysis.resource_sha256.report_templates`. All other stored measurements, findings and selected intervals must compare exactly. Corrected presentations of saved 1.0.3 JSON retain its analytical identity, with separate receipts identifying the 1.0.4 renderer. A saved-result rerender is not a numerical recomputation.
+
+## Evidence locations
+
+New public commands, raw logs and receipts use `qa/rw001/`. New public fixtures use `fixtures/reporting/rw001_report_cases.json` and synthetic source records created by the regression harness. These are explicit presentation constructions, not authorship truth or numerical oracles. No source record calculation reads scenario labels. Private corrected outputs, raw private command logs and original regression copies remain outside the source repository and public package.
+
+The production wheel/source distribution and complete source archive use new 1.0.4 destinations. Dependency locks and the digest-pinned reference-container recipe are included. No historical run is relabeled as executing this source. Actual completed commands and outcomes will be added below after their gates finish.
+
+Engineering correctness does not establish real-world authorship validity. `real_world_validation: not_established` remains unchanged.
+
+## Fresh execution results
+
+These are new 1.0.4 executions. Exact commands, timestamps, exit codes and raw stdout/stderr hashes are retained beside each receipt under `qa/rw001/`.
+
+| Check | Actual outcome | Receipt/log |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Full reference-host suite | 658 passed, 1 separate-browser skip; 110.50 s | `full-tests-final.*` |
+| RW-001 and original fixture-integrity focused gate | 15 passed; 13.72 s | `public-final.*` |
+| Original audit regressions plus adversarial reuse | 8 passed; 162.55 s, including the opt-in 1,000-record full artifact round trip | `original-audit-regressions.*` |
+| Independent reviewer component/label regressions | 24 passed; 3.16 s, including 3,000 substring-oracle comparisons | `reviewer-tests.*` |
+| Separate browser regression | 1 passed; 1.96 s | `browser-final.*` |
+| Actual synthetic HTML browser inspection | Four documents, two cases in both excerpt modes; zero remote requests or active content | `browser-review.json` |
+| Separate-process canonical replay | Six analyses: style-shift and edge fixtures under three hash seed/timezone/input-serialization settings; all 14 canonical artifacts byte-identical per fixture | `process-replay/reproduction.json` |
+| Numerical source/configuration/vendor/resource freeze | 100 existing scoped files checked, 93 byte-identical; seven exact documented metadata/template changes | `analytical-freeze.json` |
+| Historical file preservation | 890 existing archived files byte-identical, including 41 original test files, 285 old QA files and 409 old output files | `historical-preservation.json` |
+
+The public zero-primary/one-alternative regression failed before the change in both actual Markdown-to-HTML and native HTML paths because the overview was absent. The original private visibility regression is copied unchanged into the new private output tree and passes there; its historical failure receipt remains intact. The initial new public harness also had local-SVG expectations to correct. The first full run recorded three failures (the new sidecar's placement violated the original flat-fixture generator guard, and two in-flight new harness expectations were stale). The sidecar was moved to its own subdirectory and the new expectations finalized. Original tests/generator and runtime analysis were not relaxed. All failed attempts remain logged.
+
+The first build attempt used `uv build --offline` under the strict analyzer seccomp filter; uv's Unix signal socket was denied before any build. The completed build used the pinned Hatchling backend directly, under the same socket-denial filter: `.venv/bin/python scripts/offline_exec.py .venv/bin/python -m hatchling build`. This succeeded in 0.59 s. The browser's initial ambient-sandbox IPC failure is likewise retained; the successful browser run kept the dedicated internet-socket denial filter.
+
+The frozen 1.0.4 implementation fingerprint is `bfc989028bf2b47c506d1ba501287d4e362aadc25ca5c731c41e4b27a336e179`. Its report-template hash is `c8aa520917dc68d8229495a28a4375ab9e95902fe226955e79cbd1143ea3956c`. The reference identity remains `CPython-3.12.3;Linux;x86_64;numpy=2.4.2;scipy=1.17.1;ruptures=1.1.10;markdown-it-py=3.0.0;jsonschema=4.26.0`.
+
+Private current-source analysis completed in 22.32 s with 237.32 MiB peak RSS; full recomputation completed in 26.93 s with 237.91 MiB peak RSS and reproduced all 14 canonical artifacts. These are operational observations during concurrent QA, not comparative speed claims. Fresh and old analytical JSON differ at exactly the three allowed identity paths; all selected intervals and matching facts remain equal. Saved-result reports retain old analytical JSON bytes and have separately identified current renderer receipts. Public aggregate evidence omits private paths, source IDs, prose and account maps; full command logs stay beside the corrected private presentations.
+
+The actual production 1.0.4 wheel was force-installed without dependency resolution into the existing digest-identified reference image, running with `--pull never --network none`. The installed package path was checked to be `site-packages`, with the frozen current fingerprint. Its full suite passed **658 tests**, with one Chrome/Node-unavailable skip, in **144.96 s**. The separate host browser run above covers that skipped case. Installed-wheel arithmetic analysis and complete recomputation passed, and all **14 canonical artifacts** matched the source execution. See `qa/rw001/container-wheel/verification.json`, its raw per-command logs, and the outer container receipt. The old image supplies the frozen dependencies; these executions test the newly installed 1.0.4 wheel, not its bundled historical source.
+
+## Reproduce and obtain the repair
+
+```bash
+# After the exact hash-locked installation in README.md:
+python3.12 scripts/offline_exec.py .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
+python3.12 scripts/offline_exec.py .venv/bin/python -m hatchling build
+python3.12 scripts/offline_exec.py .venv/bin/python scripts/render_rw001_fixture.py --out fresh-rw001-fixtures
+python3.12 scripts/offline_exec.py .venv/bin/python scripts/check_release_reproducibility.py fresh-rw001-replay
+```
+
+The production wheel is `dist/account_history_analyzer-1.0.4-py3-none-any.whl`; source distribution is `dist/account_history_analyzer-1.0.4.tar.gz`. The full working source, locked dependencies, public regression reports and raw repair logs are in `release/1.0.4/account-history-analyzer-1.0.4-source-and-reports.tar.gz`, with its manifest/checksums beside it. The focused source/test/documentation diff is `qa/rw001/source.patch`; supporting QA scripts and raw logs are supplied in the archive. Public synthetic reports are `output/rw001/synthetic/alternative_only/report.html` and `output/rw001/synthetic/matched_plus_extra/report.html`, each with an excerpt-omitting sibling.
+
+No private input, prose or account map is included in the repair archive. Corrected private presentations and exact private command logs are supplied separately in the private task workspace. Stage B uses a separate new directory, protocol and receipts; it cannot retroactively make this pilot untouched confirmation data.
